(a) Generally. Except as hereinafter provided in subsection (b) of this section, no person shall kindle an open fire in any public or private place outside any building. Fires started in violation of this section shall be promptly extinguished by the persons responsible for the same upon notice by the chief of police, fire chief or their duly designated agent. During the existence of an air pollution alert, as may be declared by the mayor, all exceptions are void and no open fires shall be kindled.
(b) Exceptions.
(1) Open burning is authorized (for piles six feet by six feet by four feet).
a. Only leaves and limbs may be burned. Piles to be burned cannot exceed six feet by six feet by four feet high.
b. Building materials cannot be burned.
c. Stumps, tree trunks and limbs from land clearing cannot be burned.
d. Tires or other manufactured materials cannot be burned.
e. All fires must be attended at all times and extinguished if unattended.
f. All fires must be at least 50 feet from all structures.
g. All fires must be at least 25 feet from all woodlands.
h. Fires shall not be started with petroleum-based products.
i. Fires may be used for cooking of food, provided that no smoke violation or other nuisance is created.
j. Salamanders or other devices may be used for heating, provided that no smoke violation or other nuisance is created.
k. Open fires may be set for recreational purposes, such as campfires, provided that no smoke violation or other nuisance is created. The following guidelines apply to open fires:
1. Recreational fires must be at least 50 feet from all structures.
2. All recreational fires must be attended by an adult at all times.
3. A water hose or fire extinguisher must be on hand that can reach the fire, and can be ready to use if needed.
4. Recreational fires shall not be started with petroleum-based products.
5. Recreational fires must be contained in an outdoor fireplace, fire bowl, or other similar device.
(c) Open burning of vegetative material for the purpose of land clearing shall be permitted provided the following conditions are met:
(1) Authorization for such open burning is received from the city fire department chief, or the chief's designee, and turned into city hall prior to the initiation of any open burning at a location within the city limits of Glennville;
(2) The applicant must have obtained all permits required by Georgia law from the state forestry commission pursuant to O.C.G.A. § 12-6-80, et. seq., prior to applying for application hereunder;
(3) Only wood waste consisting of trees, logs, large brush and stumps which are relatively free of soil shall be burned;
(4) Tires or other rubber products, plastics, any oils, or asphaltic based or impregnated materials shall not be used to start or maintain the fire;
(5) Burning shall take place between the hours of 8:00 a.m. and 5:00 p.m. only on the day permitted. All fires must be extinguished by covering with dirt at least one hour prior to sunset on the day permitted;
(6) No burning shall take place within 50 feet from any structure, including out buildings, sheds, barns and homes;
(7) No burning shall take place within 25 feet of any adjacent woodlands, forest land, and/or open fields that contain brush, grass, or other flammable materials;
(8) The person who obtains the permit shall attend the burning at all times until completely extinguished and there is no risk for the buing to escape control;
(9) The permit holder shall take all necessary precautions to prevent escape or spread from the permitted location;
(10) The permit holder shall be liable for any resulting damage to adjacent properties;
(11) Any person who violates any provision of this article shall be subject to the provisions of section 1-11 of the Code of Ordinances of Glennville, Georgia.
(d) Open burning of understory of planted trees and/or improved pastures or cultivated crop lands. Controlled burning of the understory of planted trees and/or improved pastures or cultivated crop lands shall be permitted under the following conditions:
(1) Authorization for such controlled burning is received from the city fire department chief, or the chief's designee, and turned into city hall prior to the initiation of any controlled burning at a location within the city limits of Glennville;
(2) The applicant must have obtained all permits required by Georgia Law from the State Forestry Commission pursuant to O.C.G.A. § 12-6-80, et. seq., prior to submitting an application hereunder;
(3) Burning shall take place between the hours of 9:00 a.m. and 4:00 p.m. only on the day permitted. All fires must be extinguished no later than 4:00 p.m. on the day permitted.
(4) The location of the burning shall be no less than 50 feet from any structure, including out buildings, sheds, barns, and homes;
(5) The location of burning shall be no less than 25 feet from any woodlands, forest land, and/or open field that contains brush, grass or other flammable material;
(6) The person who obtains the permit shall attend the burning at all times until completely extinguished and there is no risk for the burning to escape control;
(7) The person responsible shall take all necessary precautions to prevent escape or spread from the permitted location;
(8) The permit holder shall be liable for any resulting damage to adjacent properties;
(9) Any person who violates any provision of this article shall be subject to the provisions of sec. 1-11 of the Code of Ordinances of Glennville, Georgia.
